  "bank": [
    {
      "id": "nda-return-copy-retention-evidence",
      "hypothesis_ids": [
        "nda-16",
        "nda-17",
        "nda-20"
      ],
      "guidance": {
        "inspection": "Separate the three tasks before deciding. For return/destruction, verify that the operative trigger is termination, expiration, abandonment, or conclusion of the contemplated relationship; a duty triggered only by request does not establish termination-triggered return. For copying, an express exception allowing necessary, automatic, or purpose-limited copies supports permission, while “no copying without consent” is a conditional prohibition rather than affirmative permission and can oppose the hypothesis; retention of an existing copy does not itself authorize creating it. For post-return possession, require an explicit retention exception, such as archival, backup, legal, regulatory, evidentiary, or professional-record retention. A command to return or destroy all originals and copies with no exception can oppose retention; silence is insufficient. Read the whole return section, including lead-ins, alternative triggers, exceptions, and continuation conditions, and inspect the complete source when search fragments omit deletion or a linked exception.",
        "source_queries": [
          {
            "all_phrases": [
