      ],
      "guidance": {
        "inspection": "Apply distinct tests. Explicit identification asks whether every protected item must be marked, designated, identified, or later confirmed by the disclosing party. A mandatory identification condition supports it; an express alternative based on circumstances, nature, or what the recipient should reasonably understand opposes the universal requirement. Mere absence of marking language does not by itself settle the issue. Technical-only scope is opposed when the definition expressly includes business, financial, marketing, customer, personnel, contractual, or other nontechnical matter; do not infer scope from an isolated generic reference. Verbal inclusion requires definition language covering oral, verbal, spoken, nonwritten, or information regardless of communication form, including any stated identification formalities. Reconstruct definitions continued across sentences or lists, retaining the governing definition and responsive categories; distinguish inclusions from exclusions and unrelated disclosure provisions. If a query returns only a fragment, inspect the complete definition in the original source.",
