      "guidance": {
        "inspection": "Decide no-grant and limited-use independently. For no-grant, look for operative language reserving ownership or rights and stating that no licence, proprietary right, or right in the protected information is conveyed. Mere access, disclosure, review, or permission to use information for an evaluation is not an unrestricted rights grant and does not by itself oppose no-grant. Conversely, an express licence must be evaluated by its object and scope: a narrow licence to background intellectual property may coexist with a broader reservation concerning confidential information. For limited use, require an operative restriction such as solely, only, exclusively, except for the stated purpose, or a prohibition on own-benefit or other-purpose use. A disclosure restriction alone is not a use restriction. Follow purpose definitions and split lead-ins, and retain qualifications or later agreements that alter the permitted use. Do not let evidence for one task determine the other; inspect the full rights and use provisions when searches return only ownership, access, or transaction language.",
